+# Sourced inspiration note
+
+## Family and source
+
+The selected family is the Japanese Kojiki tradition, situated within living Japanese religious and cultural contexts. This note makes a narrow literary comparison with one English translation; it does not characterize contemporary Shinto practice or speak for practitioners.
+
+The source is *The Kojiki*, translated by Basil Hall Chamberlain (1882), Part III, “The Door of the Heavenly Rock-Dwelling,” as transcribed by Terebess Asia Online: <https://terebess.hu/english/kojiki.html>. The delivered `kojiki-excerpt.md` contains four short excerpt lines from that passage.
+
+## Motif-level comparison
+
+The passage describes the land darkening and eternal night (excerpt lines 1-2), a divine assembly gathering (excerpt lines 3-3), and the land becoming light again after the deity comes forth (excerpt lines 4-4). I use only that high-level sequence as a prompt for the independently invented first Night Mark, Wind Court assembly, and raised Beacon in this fixture. This is an editorial analogy, not a claim that the fixture's factions, light, or night-mark are traditional symbols or meanings. No named deity, place, ritual, or plot is imported as fixture canon.
+
+## Attribution — open question
+
+The text-level reference is attributed to *The Kojiki*, translated by Basil Hall Chamberlain, 1882, Part III, “The Door of the Heavenly Rock-Dwelling,” at the linked transcription above. This attribution identifies the selected work, translator, section, and online transcription; it does not establish a single ancient author or settle rights.
+
+## Rights — open question
+
+The rights status of the Terebess transcription, its presentation, and any editorial work has not been determined. The age of Chamberlain's translation does not by itself settle the status of this online transcription in every jurisdiction. Check applicable site terms and rights information before reuse beyond this short research excerpt.
+
+## AI-training permission — open question
+
+AI-training permission has not been assessed or established. Public accessibility, attribution, or any eventual copyright conclusion does not answer whether training use is permitted; that question remains separate and open.
